| 4 | -0.90 | -0.60 | 0.91 |  |
| 5 | 0.00 | 0.10 | 0.04 |  |
| 6 | 1.50 | 1.50 | 2.43 |  |
| 7 | 1.50 | -1.50 | 30.00 | yes |
| 8 | -0.40 | -0.10 | 0.37 |  |
| 9 | 1.40 | -1.30 | 24.30 | yes |
\image(figures/hotelling_chart.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Squared Mahalanobis distances from the calibrated two-sensor reference.)
\alttext(The Hotelling chart flags observations with opposing sensor deviations, including run seven.)

> Interpret the chart

Compare runs six and seven. Each sensor is 1.5 individual standard
deviations from its target in both runs, but the signs differ in run seven.
The squared Mahalanobis distance accounts for the positive covariance and
flags that combination. The chi-squared limit here applies to independent
multivariate Normal readings with a known reference mean and covariance.

> References

- [NIST Hotelling control charts|https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmc/section3/pmc341.htm]
